<%- include('partials/header') %>
<section class="page-banner" style="--banner: url('/img/miners-underground.jpg')">
  <div class="wrap">
    <nav class="crumbs"><a href="/">Home</a> / <span>How it works</span></nav>
    <h1>How it works</h1>
    <p>What to expect when you buy or sell through us.</p>
  </div>
</section>
<div class="wrap two-col how">
  <section class="panel">
    <h2>If you're buying</h2>
    <ol class="mini-steps">
      <li><strong>Browse and shortlist.</strong> Filter by mineral, province, size, price and paperwork. Save anything that catches your eye.</li>
      <li><strong>Ask us anything.</strong> Use the enquiry form on the listing. We'll come back with what we know and what we don't.</li>
      <li><strong>Visit the site.</strong> Request a site visit and we'll arrange access with the owner. Bring your own geologist if you like, and take your own samples to an accredited lab.</li>
      <li><strong>Check the title.</strong> We'll share the registration paperwork so you can verify it at the Provincial Mining Director's office.</li>
      <li><strong>Make an offer.</strong> Click “Buy or make an offer” on the listing. We take it to the owner and you'll see the answer on your dashboard.</li>
      <li><strong>Sign and transfer.</strong> Once you agree, the sale goes through a written agreement and the official transfer process, ideally with a lawyer. Money only moves under that agreement.</li>
    </ol>
    <a class="btn" href="/mines">Start browsing</a>
  </section>
  <section class="panel">
    <h2>If you're selling</h2>
    <ol class="mini-steps">
      <li><strong>Create an account</strong> so buyers know there's a real person behind the listing.</li>
      <li><strong>Tell us about the property.</strong> Location, size, grades, what's on site and what paperwork exists. Add photos if you have them.</li>
      <li><strong>We review it.</strong> Our team checks every submission, usually within two working days. If we need more, we'll tell you exactly what.</li>
      <li><strong>Go live.</strong> Once approved, your listing appears on the site and you can follow its views from your dashboard.</li>
      <li><strong>We screen buyers.</strong> Enquiries and offers come through us first, so you don't waste time on people who aren't serious.</li>
      <li><strong>Close the deal.</strong> We help line up the site visit, the meeting and the paperwork.</li>
    </ol>
    <a class="btn" href="/sell">List your mine</a>
  </section>
</div>
<div class="wrap section faq-section">
  <div class="faq-list">
  <h2>Common questions</h2>
  <details class="faq"><summary>Can I pay for a mine through the website?</summary><p>No. Mining rights are a big purchase and transfer has to go through the proper legal process. The website handles the introduction, enquiries and offers. Payment happens under a signed sale agreement, preferably through a lawyer's trust account or escrow.</p></details>
  <details class="faq"><summary>What does “Direct Mandate” mean?</summary><p>It means the person who gave us the listing has been authorised directly by the owner to sell. A “New Mandate” is a recently received instruction we're still getting the full pack together for.</p></details>
  <details class="faq"><summary>Are the grades guaranteed?</summary><p>No. Grades come from the seller's own sampling and reports. Always take your own samples and use an independent lab before you commit.</p></details>
  <details class="faq"><summary>Why was my listing declined?</summary><p>Usually something's missing, like the location, a realistic size or some sign you're entitled to sell. Your dashboard shows our note. Fix it, resubmit, and we'll look again.</p></details>
  <details class="faq"><summary>Can foreigners buy mining claims in Zimbabwe?</summary><p>Often yes, but rules on ownership, indigenisation and certain minerals (like lithium ore exports) change from time to time. Talk to a Zimbabwean mining lawyer about your situation before you make an offer.</p></details>
  </div>
  <img class="faq-photo" src="/img/site-inspection.jpg" alt="Two mine surveyors in hard hats checking a tablet on a working pit face" width="597" height="335">
</div>
